asian migrant guadalupe  shows us the shack where she and her  family have been living for two weeks  since arriving in this colombian beach  town  there is no running water look this is  what we have for 12 people all crammed  here spending eight dollars a day  we have kids who need to eat prices of  food have doubled since we arrived and  we had no plans to stay here for weeks  she's one of nearly 22 000 haitians  mostly arriving from brazil in chile who  are holed up waiting to catch a boat  toward the dangerous jungle of the  durian gap to continue their journey  north but panama is allowing only 500  migrants to enter each day  a human bottleneck that has brought the  town's infrastructure to a breaking  point but has also revitalized an  economy devastated by covid 19 mostly to  detriment of the migrants  haitians are paying premium prices for  food and other basic items often being  forced to pay in dollars  a cottage industry has grown to respond  to their needs  stalls like this one have appeared all  over town selling everything the  migrants need to cross the jungle from  sleeping mats to boots  raincoats headlights portable kitchens  and even special bombs that people hear  say protect you from poisonous snakes  over a hundred families have been  expelled from their apartments by  landlords hoping to make quick cash like  mechanic victor lopez who has lost his  house and workshop and is now working on  the street and living with his daughter  they kicked me out to make space for the  haitians from one day to another and  gave me no time to find an alternative  ismail guevara is hosting up to 30  migrants per day in his home which he is  now expanding he rejects accusations  that is taking advantage of people's  despair saying he's providing a service  it's a mix of opportunities and needs  they need a safe place to stay  with their women and children a roof a  kitchen where they can cook and we open  the doors of our homes yes we earn money  but not everyone opens its doors to  migrants  fearing she'll end up having to pitch a  tent on the beach guadalupe spends every  day hoping to change her boat tickets  scheduled for october 18th to an earlier  date  but she says it's all worth it to offer  her family a better future  all i want is to reach a place where i  can live safely and freely where my  children can study and make a life for  themselves  colombian officials believe that  president biden's efforts to deport  haitians from the u.s will halt the flow  of migrants through nekkoclei but for  now their numbers keep growing  alessandra  al jazeera
 

